"Works forward from an initiating event through successive protection successes or failures to distinct consequence sequences.\n\nUse when: Examine how recovery measures change the outcome of a disturbance. Review combinations of protective system responses after one trigger.\n\nAvoid when: The main question is the causal combinations that create a failure.\n\nVisual grammar: A starting event anchors the scenario. Ordered branch points represent specific protective functions succeeding or failing. Terminal paths identify distinct recovery or harm outcomes.\n\nBuild: Define the initiating event and credible operating conditions. Order the protective responses and state each success criterion. Expand feasible sequences and assign outcomes, excluding impossible branches explicitly.\n\nCheck: Branches reflect conditional responses in their actual sequence. Every retained path leads to a defined consequence. Any probabilities are conditional and avoid unsupported independence assumptions.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a barrier as available after its prerequisite has failed. Adding branch probabilities without explaining the conditioning.\n\nExample: After a freezer alarm, staff notification, response and backup cooling determine whether contents are preserved or lost.\n\nAlternatives: Fault tree, Bow-tie diagram."
